CELLS = {
    0: '# Notebook 06 — Predicting Synthesis Crystallization: An End-to-End Data Science Case Study\n\n**Project:** ALCheMy · **Author:** Rubén Serrano Nieto & AI Agent Pair · **Date:** 2026-09-02 (v4)\n\n---\n\n### How to Read This Notebook\n\nThis notebook walks through **a data science problem from start to finish**: from "what laboratory question do I want to answer?" to an evaluated model and a feature importance analysis of the underlying chemical variables. It is not merely a script that trains a model; it is the log of why every decision was made as it was, **including those that failed**.\n\nIt is structured according to the **10-stage methodological framework**, the most widely adopted refinement of **CRISP-DM** (*Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining*, 1999, still the de facto standard today). Both convey the same concepts at different granularities; the 10-stage framework better disentangles the early phases, which is where research projects like this succeed or fail.\n\n| Stage (10 stages) | CRISP-DM Phase | Where is it here? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Problem Understanding | Business Understanding | §1 |\n| 2. Analytic Approach | Business Understanding | §2 |\n| 3. Data Requirements | Data Understanding | §3 |\n| 4. Data Collection | Data Understanding | §4 |\n| 5. Data Understanding | Data Understanding | §5 (EDA) |\n| 6. Data Preparation | Data Preparation | §6 (ETL) |\n| 7. Modeling | Modeling | §7 |\n| 8. Evaluation | Evaluation | §8 |\n| 9. Deployment | Deployment | §9 |\n| 10. Feedback | Deployment | §10 |\n\n**The workflow is not a linear list; it is a feedback loop**, and this notebook is proof: it is on its third iteration. The first reached stage 8 with a seemingly good result; a chemical sanity check sent it back to stages 3 and 6. The second resolved that issue and failed again, this time due to a **units error** that could only be caught by reading the original Excel sheet formulas.\n\n> **Note on figures.** Labels, axes, and titles are written in English (scientific publication convention); technical reasoning is in Spanish.\n\n### Table of Contents\n\n- **§1** Problem Definition · **§2** Classification vs. Regression · **§3** Data Requirements\n- **§4** Data Lineage & Provenance · **§5** EDA · **§6** Data Preparation & ETL\n- **§7** Modeling · **§8** Evaluation · **§9** Deployment & Registry · **§10** Feedback Loop\n',
    1: '---\n\n> ### 📌 What Changed in This Version: The Units Discrepancy\n>\n> The previous version assumed that **all** additive columns in the master Excel sheet were in microliters. They were not. The evidence was in the spreadsheet itself, inside the formula of the `V total` column:\n>\n> ```\n> V total = SUMA(Q:AB) + SUMA(AE:AU)/1000\n> ```\n>\n> That `/1000` applied **only to the second block** proves that the first block is in **milliliters**. And it makes full laboratory sense: **dilute** solutions (HCl 0.6 M, HCl 3 M, HF 3 M) are dosed in mL and **act as solvents**; concentrated solutions and pure liquids are dosed in µL.\n>\n> | Block | Columns | Unit |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | **Q:AB** | **HCl 0,6M · HCl 3M · HF 3M** · H₂O · MeOH · EtOH · 1-PrOH · DMF · DEF · AcOH gl · H₂PO₄ · NH₄OH | **mL** |\n> | **AE:AU** | Formic · Acetic · Propionic · TEA · CF₃SO₃H · TFA · HCl 1M/5M/12M · KOH 10M · HF 3M (2nd) · MeO₃S… | **µL** |\n>\n> **The consequence was severe and silent:** by dividing dilute solutions by 1,000, **the water they contributed vanished**. In SMP-0019, 10 mL of HCl 0.6 M became 0.010 mL. That is why volumes "did not balance" in 18% of syntheses and those rows were marked as unknown composition. No solvent was missing: **dilution water was missing**.\n>\n> Once corrected, medium breakdown coverage increases from **81.7%** of combinatorial syntheses to **99.5%** — and to **99.8%** in the training set. (Across all 2,152 syntheses of all types, including standard and microwave, it is 99.1%: the denominator matters and is stated explicitly.) And to state plainly: **the model does not improve as a result** (macro F1 0.517 vs. 0.522 previously, a difference seven times smaller than the cross-fold standard deviation, ±0.036). What was gained is not higher accuracy, but **that the numbers mean what they say**.\n>\n> Verification: the formula reproduces the stored `V total` across **2,162 of 2,172 rows (99.54%)**.\n',
    2: "---\n\n# §1. Problem Understanding\n\n> *Stage 1 — Business Understanding. What we want to achieve, phrased in the domain owner's language. If this stage is flawed, everything downstream is well-executed on the wrong problem.*\n\n### The Laboratory Question\n\nSynthesizing a phosphonate MOF takes **between 24 and 96 hours in an oven**, plus the time required for weighing, assembly, and characterization. Out of every 24 wells on a combinatorial synthesis plate, the majority fail to crystallize. The question is straightforward:\n\n> **Can we predict, *before* placing anything in the oven, whether a given combination of synthetic conditions will yield crystalline material?**\n\n### Concrete Utility\n\n1. **Discard** doomed combinations before spending 48 h of oven time.\n2. **Prioritize** the next batch: if a plate holds 24 wells, fill them with the 24 most promising candidates rather than a blind grid search.\n3. **Understand** what governs crystallization in this chemical family — which is ultimately the most valuable outcome: an accurate model that cannot explain teaches nothing.\n\n### Success Criteria, Established BEFORE Inspecting Results\n\nThis is not a formality: it is the only defense against cherry-picking flattering metrics later.\n\n- **Minimum acceptable:** clearly outperform the majority-class baseline in **macro F1**.\n- **Truly useful:** candidate ranking populates a plate significantly better than random guessing.\n- **Non-negotiable:** the model must be **interpretable in chemical terms**.\n",
    3: '---\n\n# §2. Analytic Approach\n\n> *Stage 2 — Analytic Approach. Translating the laboratory question into a statistical problem type. A wrong choice here cannot be salvaged downstream with better models.*\n\n| If the question is… | Then… |\n|---|---|\n| Which **category** does it belong to? | **Supervised Classification** |\n| What **numerical value** will it take? | Supervised Regression |\n| Are there **natural clusters** without labels? | Clustering (Unsupervised) |\n| Can I **compress** many columns? | Dimensionality Reduction (PCA…) |\n\n### Why Classification Rather Than Regression\n\nThis is the most crucial design decision in the notebook, and could have gone either way. A reasonable candidate for a continuous target exists: `amorphous_fraction_blank`, the fraction of scattered intensity that was diffuse rather than Bragg peaks — a number between 0 and 1 that *seems* to quantify "how crystalline" the material is.\n\n**It was ruled out for three reasons, ordered by importance:**\n\n1. **The continuous variable is unreliable at its extrema.** It exhibits a detection floor of ~0.02 (notebook 05 §7.5): below that threshold is baseline noise, not signal. A regression model would treat the difference between 0.005 and 0.015 as real information when both represent "nothing measurable". The CRYSTALLINE class lives entirely in that blind zone.\n2. **Laboratory decisions are categorical.** No chemist decides whether to repeat a synthesis based on whether the amorphous fraction is 0.42 or 0.48; the decision is based on whether it crystallized, partially crystallized, or remained amorphous. **The model must predict the actual decision to be made.**\n3. **Ground truth is a categorical human judgment**: three discrete classes, not a continuum.\n\n> **On metrics.** **MRE**, RMSE, and R² belong to **regression**: they measure distance from a continuous target. In classification there is no distance metric: the class is either correctly assigned or not. The proper equivalents are **macro F1, MCC, and balanced accuracy**, detailed in §8.1.\n\n### The Three Target Classes\n\n| Class | Physical Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **AMORFO** | Diffuse halo only: no long-range crystalline order |\n| **INCIPIENTE** | Weak Bragg peaks over a dominant diffuse halo: partial ordering began |\n| **CRISTALINO** | Sharp, well-defined Bragg diffraction peaks |\n\nThey are **ordinal** (amorphous < incipient < crystalline), which becomes important when interpreting classification errors in §8.3.\n',
    4: '---\n\n# §3. Data Requirements\n\n> *Stage 3 — Data Requirements. What is ideally required, declared BEFORE inspecting available data. Doing it in reverse leads to rationalizing whatever data happens to be on hand.*\n\n| Block | Ideal Variables | Available? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Metal** | Identity, oxidation state, ionic radius, hardness, oxophilicity | ✅ Engineered (§4.2) |\n| **Linker** | Backbone, denticity, length, protection state | ✅ Engineered (§4.2) |\n| **Stoichiometry** | [M], L/M ratio | ✅ In master Excel |\n| **Medium** | Solvent composition breakdown | ✅ **Complete after units correction (§6.1)** |\n| | **pH** | ❌ **Never recorded** |\n| **Modulator** | Quantity and type (acidity, polarity) | ✅ After §6 corrections |\n| **Process** | Temperature, duration, ramps | ✅ In master Excel |\n| | **Autogenous pressure** | ❌ Not measured |\n| **Outcome** | Crystallinity class | ✅ Manually labeled |\n\n### Known Gaps, Stated Upfront\n\n- **pH is missing.** In phosphonate chemistry, this is likely **the most important missing variable**: it dictates the deprotonation state of the linker and thus its coordination capacity. All downstream analysis proceeds acknowledging its absence.\n- **Autogenous pressure is also missing**, though it is partially a function of temperature, fill volume, and solvent composition, which are present.\n\nDeclaring them upfront carries a concrete consequence: when a process variable shows low importance in §8, we must distinguish between *"it does not matter"* and *"it was not measured"*.\n',
    5: '---\n\n# §4. Data Collection\n\n> *Stage 4 — Data Collection. Data lineage, provenance, and domain features engineered from scratch.*\n\n### 4.1 Data Lineage in ALCheMy: From Lab Notebook to Model\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph G1["(1) Raw Sources"]\n        A1["Master Synthesis Excel<br/>(5 years of lab notebooks)"]\n        A2["~5,700 Instrument Files<br/>(.xrdml .brml .txt .csv .xls .jpg)"]\n    end\n    subgraph G2["(2) Ingestion"]\n        B1["notebook 01: header mapping, stoichiometric engineering,<br/>thermal ramp parsing, MD5 natural key, UPSERT"]\n        B2["deterministic parsers<br/>(common contract: header_metadata + raw_data)"]\n    end\n    subgraph G3["(3) Storage"]\n        C1[("synthesis_experiments<br/>1 row / synthesis")]\n        C2[("characterization_measurements<br/>1 row / measurement, JSONB + GIN")]\n    end\n    D1["(4) notebook 05: arPLS, halo subtraction, peak detection"]\n    D2[("pxrd_descriptors")]\n    D3["app/pxrd_labeler.py (human judgment)"]\n    D4>"pxrd_labels — TARGET LABEL (Y)"]\n    E1["(5) element_properties + ion_properties + linker_properties"]\n    E2[("v_ml_features — ML FEATURES (X)")]\n    F1["(6) This notebook: §5 EDA → §6 ETL → §7 modeling → §8 evaluation"]\n    F2{{"(7) Domain Expert Review<br/>returns workflow to §3 and §6"}}\n\n    A1 --> B1 --> C1\n    A2 --> B2 --> C2\n    C2 -->|FK| C1\n    C2 --> D1 --> D2 --> D3 --> D4\n    C1 --> E1 --> E2\n    C1 --> E2\n    D4 --> F1\n    E2 --> F1\n    F1 --> F2\n    F2 -.->|fixes| B1\n    F2 -.->|fixes| E2\n\n    classDef raw fill:#ECEFF1,stroke:#78909C,color:#263238\n    classDef proc fill:#F1F8E9,stroke:#7CB342,color:#33691E\n    classDef store fill:#E3F2FD,stroke:#1E88E5,color:#0D47A1\n    classDef target fill:#FFF3E0,stroke:#FB8C00,color:#E65100\n    classDef human fill:#FCE4EC,stroke:#D81B60,color:#880E4F\n    class A1,A2 raw\n    class B1,B2,D1,D3 proc\n    class C1,C2,D2,E1 store\n    class D4,E2 target\n    class F2 human\n```\n\n**The feedback arrows are not decorative.** They represent what has already occurred twice.\n\n### 4.2 Engineering Chemical Descriptors\n\nThe raw Excel stores the metal as **text** (`"Zr"`) and the linker as **text** (`"LIG-D"`). An ML model can do nothing with raw strings except memorize names.\n\nThe naive alternative is *one-hot encoding*: one dummy column per metal. This was rejected on chemical grounds: **one-hot implies that Zr and Hf are as dissimilar as Zr and Ca**. They are not — they are chemical near-twins due to the lanthanide contraction. With one-hot encoding, a model cannot transfer knowledge learned on Zr to Hf, let alone generalize to an unseen metal.\n\n| Table | Information Provided |\n|---|---|\n| `element_properties` | Electronegativity, hardness, polarizability, oxophilicity |\n| `ion_properties` | Ionic radius and charge/radius ratio **by oxidation state** (Shannon, CN=6) |\n| `metal_source_oxidation_state` | Oxidation state implied by precursor salt formula |\n| `linker_properties` | Backbone, protection, denticity, donor-to-donor distance |\n| `property_sources` | Literature citations and DOIs for every property |\n\n**Two deliberate design choices:**\n\n- **Oxidation state is derived from precursor formula**, not the element\'s "most common" state. Iron enters as Fe(II) or Fe(III), cerium as Ce(III) or Ce(IV), which substantially shifts ionic radius and charge density.\n- **Sparse, hand-curated linker descriptors.** RDKit offers >200 descriptors. There are only **4 distinct linkers**. Having more descriptors than molecules causes memorization, not learning.\n',
    6: '---\n\n# §5. Data Understanding (EDA)\n\n> *Stage 5 — Data Understanding. Exploring the data before modeling. It yields the highest return per hour invested, yet is the most frequently skipped.*\n\nBefore loading any data, **the decision that defines the dataset scope**.\n',
    7: '### 5.0 Synthetic Scope: The Confounder That Nearly Distorted the Analysis\n\nHistorical records contain three synthesis methodologies, which the first version of this notebook naively blended:\n\n| Type | AMORFO | CRISTALINO | INCIPIENTE | **% Crystalline** |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| **HTS** — High-Throughput Screening | 401 | 169 | 971 | **11.0%** |\n| **ST** — Standard Solvothermal | 3 | 71 | 15 | **79.8%** |\n| **MW** — Microwave-Assisted | 0 | 90 | 4 | **95.7%** |\n\n**Zero amorphous samples in microwave synthesis.** A model trained on the pooled data discovers a trivial shortcut: if it detects — even via reactor volume or synthesis year — that an experiment is microwave-assisted, predicting CRYSTALLINE yields 96% accuracy.\n\n**This is not a chemical method effect: it is selection bias.** Combinatorial HTS is *exploratory* and therefore dominated by failed crystallizations; standard solvothermal and microwave methods are used **once the recipe is already known to work**. The "synthesis type" feature largely encodes *"did the chemist already know this would crystallize?"*. Predicting that is historical record-keeping, not chemistry.\n\nThere is also an **independent thermal physics argument**: microwave irradiation heats rapidly and volumetrically, favoring kinetic phases over thermodynamic ones. The relationship between synthetic conditions and product outcome **is not the same physical function** in a microwave as in an oven.\n\n> **We train exclusively on High-Throughput Screening (HTS) syntheses.** It is the only subset free from success bias, matches the actual target deployment scenario (the next experimental plate is an HTS plate), and operates under a uniform thermal regime. **The tradeoff is stated plainly:** we sacrifice 90 of the 330 crystalline cases. An honest, lower F1 score is far more valuable than an inflated metric that merely detects synthesis methodology.\n',
    9: "### 5.1 The SQL Extraction Query: Inclusions, Exclusions, and Rationale\n\n| Filter | Rationale |\n|---|---|\n| `synthesis_type = 'HTS'` | §5.0: isolates unbiased exploratory screening with uniform thermal physics |\n| `label_value <> 'SIN_MUESTRA'` | No physical material to classify: represents missing experimental output, not a class |\n| `ml_include = 'NO'` excluded | Omits records flagged during expert review (duplicates, oxide artifacts) — §6.3 |\n| `MEZCLA → CRISTALINO` | Phase mixtures contain crystalline material; for §1's question, crystallization occurred |\n| `linker <> 'Tannic acid'` | §6.4 — non-phosphonate outlier detailed below |\n",
    11: '**1 row = 1 physical sample.** The `assert` verification is essential: in the first version of the notebook, this condition **failed** (43 samples had duplicate labeled measurements), prompting the deduplication protocol in §6.3.\n',
    12: '### 5.2 Data Profiling: Column Data Types and Cardinality\n\nThe initial step in exploratory data analysis is systematic: inspecting data types, missing value counts, and unique values per column to identify anomalies early.\n',
    14: '### 5.3 Missing Data Analysis: Mechanisms and Why Naive Imputation Fails\n\nAn aggregate "9% missing values" figure can mask distinct underlying mechanisms. A missing entry can represent *«not measured»*, *«does not exist»*, or *«unmatched string identifier»*, and only the first aligns with standard imputation assumptions.\n',
    17: '**Five distinct missingness mechanisms, five tailored solutions.**\n\n| Property | Affected Species | Missingness Mechanism | Handling Protocol |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `electronegativity_allen` | **Ce, La, Dy, Nd** | The Allen scale is spectroscopic and **undefined for lanthanides** | **Drop column.** Not a missing entry: physically undefined. Pauling scale covers all 20 metals |\n| `absolute_hardness_ev` | **Mg, Zn** | η = (IP−EA)/2; filled shells **exhibit no electron affinity** | Impute with median, acknowledging approximation |\n| `oxophilicity_kepp` | **Ga** | **Absent** in original reference table | Documented permanent null |\n| `mod_carbox_mean_pka` | ~69% of rows | **Not a missing value:** synthesis uses no carboxylic modulator; "no acid" has no pKa | Impute; explicit missingness indicator tested without benefit |\n| `frac_*` (solvents) | **0.2%** of rows | After unit correction, only 3 rows exhibit volume imbalances | Retain as NULL, not 0 |\n\n**The last row reflects the update in this version.** Previously, 18% of rows had missing solvent breakdowns, attributed to "incomplete volumetric logs". **That diagnosis was incorrect**: the breakdown was present, but dilution water had been divided by 1,000 upon ingestion. Correcting units eliminated virtually all missingness.\n\nThe underlying principle remains critical, now applied to just 3 rows: **imputing 0 for an unknown value asserts a false statement.** Telling a model "this synthesis contained zero methanol" when the truth is "methanol volume is unrecorded" introduces structured misinformation.\n',
    18: '### 5.4 Target Variable Distribution: Class Imbalance\n\nClass imbalance directly dictates evaluation metrics (§8.1), cross-validation strategy, and model suitability.\n',
    20: '**That final baseline number is the benchmark to bear in mind throughout this notebook.** A trivial majority-class classifier achieves ~64% accuracy. Reporting "64% accuracy" would masquerade as predictive performance while delivering zero signal.\n\nThe class imbalance is **5.8:1**, more pronounced than before filtering to HTS because excluded solvothermal/microwave runs were predominantly crystalline. We mitigate this using **cost-sensitive learning** (`class_weight=\'balanced\'`), which penalizes minority class errors without synthesizing artificial data.\n\n**(b)** reveals key domain signal: the fraction of crystalline outcomes **varies substantially across metals**, confirming genuine chemical signal.\n',
    21: '### 5.5 Distribution of Numerical Features\n\nWith ~35 numerical features, visual inspection via grid plots reveals continuous distributions, **disguised discrete variables**, and skewed features.\n',
    23: "**Two structural insights that condition downstream modeling:**\n\n1. **Process variables are discrete de facto**: `temp_target_c` has ~13 distinct values, `time_hours` ~9, `lm_ratio` ~9. This is not a defect: it reflects combinatorial screening protocols — fixing a discrete grid and sweeping composition. However, it carries a key implication revisited in §8.4: **a feature with narrow variance cannot explain broad target variance, regardless of its underlying chemical importance.**\n\n2. **`reactor_volume_ml` has a SINGLE constant value** (2.5 mL). It is constant in this subset: zero variance. It provides zero signal and appears blank in §5.6's correlation heatmap — not due to missing data, but because **correlation with zero variance is undefined (0/0)**. Since `fill_fraction = total_volume_ml / reactor_volume_ml`, these columns represent **the same variable rescaled**: perfect collinearity. Both redundancies are removed in §6.5.\n\n(`lm_ratio` is the **theoretical linker-to-metal molar ratio**, Excel's `L/M` column: range 0.1–1.0, median 0.50. It is set during initial reactant dispensing, making it **directly actionable** in the laboratory.)\n",
    24: '### 5.6 Correlation Analysis: Feature Collinearity vs. Target Association\n\nThe correlation heatmap compares all feature pairs to identify **linear relationships**. It serves two distinct objectives:\n\n**(1) Collinearity among input features.** The objective is **not zero correlation**, but *identifying where strong dependencies exist*. High collinearity indicates redundant information, leading to three consequences:\n\n- **Impact on feature interpretability** (crucial here): if ionic radius and charge/radius ratio are collinear, tree models split importance arbitrarily between them. Concluding "this variable is unimportant" would be incorrect: it matters, but its twin absorbs the split credit. Hence, §8.4 interprets importance **by chemical functional blocks**.\n- **Impact on linear models**: logistic regression becomes numerically unstable under multicollinearity, producing erratic, high-magnitude coefficients.\n- **Data leakage detection**: high correlation between ostensibly unrelated variables often reveals unintended feature derivation. **Observed here**: `fill_fraction` and `total_volume_ml` correlate at exactly 1.0 due to constant reactor volume (§5.5).\n\nStrong collinear blocks represent physical relationships — properties of the same metal cation correlating **by underlying physics**, not data error.\n\n> **Blank heatmap cells do not denote missing data.** They indicate zero-variance features: `reactor_volume_ml` is fixed at 2.5 mL, where Pearson correlation evaluates to 0/0 (undefined).\n\n**(2) Feature association with the target.** This represents the actual signal exploited by classifiers. Pearson r is inapplicable to 3-class categorical targets. Appropriate metrics are the **correlation ratio η²** (numerical vs. categorical) and **Cramér\'s V** (categorical vs. categorical).\n',
    27: "**Interpreting (b).** η² answers: *what fraction of this feature's total variance is explained by knowing the target class?* Zero indicates identical distributions across amorphous and crystalline outcomes — providing zero standalone discriminant signal.\n\n> ⚠️ **Correlation does not imply causation.** These associations simply measure joint variation. A statistical link between temperature and crystallinity **does not prove** that increasing temperature causes crystallization: they may share a confounding factor (higher-temperature plates may have utilized a different linker), or reflect historical screening bias. Establishing causality requires **controlled experimental intervention** — holding all else constant while perturbing temperature —, which is precisely what the model *suggests where to test*, not what it proves.\n",
    28: '### 5.7 Bivariate Class Separability\n\nWhile §5.6 provides scalar association metrics, boxplots visualize **how** feature distributions separate across the three classes.\n',
    30: '**No single feature achieves standalone class separation.** Distributions overlap across all three categories. This demonstrates that crystallization is an intrinsically **multivariate problem**, justifying machine learning over heuristic univariate thresholds.\n',
    31: '### 5.8 Group Structure and Data Leakage: Why Random Splits Fail\n\nThe most critical insight of exploratory data analysis: **observations in this dataset are not independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.).**\n',
    33: '**(a)** Syntheses are processed in **well plates**: batches prepared on the same day, sharing the same linker, thermal profile, and often metal salt, sweeping compositional ratios.\n\n**(b)** Well plates exhibit high **internal homogeneity**: knowing the outcome of one well strongly predicts neighboring wells on the same plate.\n\n**The modeling consequence is critical.** A naive random train/test split would allocate wells from the *same plate* across both partitions. The model would not learn transferable chemistry; it would merely identify plate-level batch signatures. **Reported test performance would be artificially inflated and misleading.** Therefore, §7.2 enforces grouped cross-validation using `StratifiedGroupKFold` grouped by plate ID.\n\n**(c)** In addition, the dataset exhibits **temporal drift**. Sample counts are annotated on each bar: proportions without sample sizes are easily misinterpreted.\n',
    34: '### 5.9 Global Geometric Structure: PCA Projection\n\nAn unsupervised diagnostic visualization before training. **This is an exploratory diagnostic, not a dimensionality reduction model.**\n\n#### Construction Protocol\n\n1. **Standardize** numerical features (mean 0, variance 1) post-imputation. Otherwise, `time_hours` (up to 96) would dominate `frac_h2o` (0–1) purely due to unit magnitude, reflecting arbitrary units rather than chemical variance.\n2. **Compute principal directions of maximal variance.** PC1 represents a **linear combination of all input features** maximizing explained variance; PC2 represents the orthogonal direction maximizing remaining variance.\n3. **Project** observations onto the principal plane for visualization.\n\n**PCA does not select or discard individual variables**: it utilizes the full feature set without class labels (**unsupervised**). Class colors are overlaid post-projection, ensuring an honest diagnostic of natural cluster separability.\n',
    36: '**(a)** Latent structure exists, but **classes exhibit significant spatial overlap**: confirming a complex, non-linearly separable multivariate landscape. Marker shapes indicate that linker backbones occupy distinct subregions — linker chemistry accounts for substantial variance in the feature space.\n\n**(b)** Adding PC3 reveals no hidden linear separation. This serves as a **valuable negative diagnostic**: no hyperplane cleanly segregates the classes, indicating that linear classifiers will struggle.\n\n**(c)** Retaining 90% of total variance requires numerous principal components: information is **distributed across many orthogonal dimensions**. Applying PCA dimensionality reduction prior to modeling would **discard predictive signal**; hence, **PCA is omitted from the production pipeline** and retained solely as an exploratory diagnostic.\n',
    37: '---\n\n# §6. Data Preparation (ETL)\n\n> *Stage 6 — Data Preparation. The most labor-intensive phase of any applied project, and where expert review twice redirected the workflow.*\n\nAll transformations below represent reproducible database-level updates executed during this analysis, preserving complete audit lineage.\n',
    38: '### 6.1 Volumetric Unit Correction: Detection and Proof\n\nThis revision resolved a subtle data ingestion defect that **no automated sanity check could have flagged**.\n\n**The Symptom.** In 18% of synthesis records, component solvent volumes failed to sum to the recorded total volume. Previous iterations attributed this to incomplete laboratory logging and imputed missing solvent fractions.\n\n**The Physical Inconsistency.** SMP-0035 specifies a 15 mL reaction volume, while the database recorded 15 **µL** of water. This represents a physical impossibility rather than an omission: a 15 mL hydrothermal autoclave reaction containing 15 µL of water reflects an ingestion error.\n\n**The Evidence.** The master Excel spreadsheet formula for `V total` explicitly revealed the unit structure:\n\n```\nV total = SUMA(Q:AB) + SUMA(AE:AU)/1000\n```\n\nThe `/1000` divisor applied **only to the second block** proves that the first block is recorded in **milliliters**. This formula exactly reproduces the stored total volume across **2,162 of 2,172 rows (99.54%)**.\n\n| Block | Columns | Unit |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Q:AB** | **HCl 0,6M · HCl 3M · HF 3M** · H₂O · MeOH · EtOH · 1-PrOH · DMF · DEF · AcOH gl · H₂PO₄ · NH₄OH | **mL** |\n| **AE:AU** | Formic · Acetic · Propionic · TEA · CF₃SO₃H · TFA · HCl 1M/5M/12M · KOH 10M · HF 3M (2nd) · MeO₃S… | **µL** |\n\nThis aligns with experimental protocol: **dilute** mineral acid solutions are dosed in milliliters and **act as primary solvents**; concentrated acids and pure modulators are dosed in microliters.\n\n**The Impact.** Notebook 01 previously divided **all** additive columns by 1,000, causing:\n\n1. **Dilution water to vanish.** In SMP-0019, 10 mL of 0.6 M HCl was ingested as 0.010 mL, explaining the 18% volume discrepancy.\n2. `hcl_active_mmol` and `hf_active_mmol` were underestimated by a **factor of 1,000** for those columns.\n3. Three columns in the mL block (**glacial AcOH, H₂PO₄, NH₄OH**) were mapped in notebook 01 but **omitted from the SQL schema**, dropping their volumes.\n4. Duplicate headers for `HF (3M)` (70 rows) and `TFA` (6 rows) were dropped by pandas due to automatic `.1` suffixing.\n\n**What was NOT incorrect:** pure organic liquid modulators (formic, acetic, propionic, TFA, triflic, triethylamine, methanesulfonic) are indeed dosed in µL; their prior stoichiometric derivations remain valid.\n\n**The Fix**, implemented in `src/fix_volume_units_2026_09.py` and `scripts/sql/migrations/2026-09-03_ml_features_v3.sql`: recalculate each column with verified unit conversions, restore missing solvent columns, and aggregate duplicate headers. Glacial acetic acid is dual-accounted as both solvent volume and carboxylic modulator millimoles.\n',
    40: '**Medium solvent breakdown sums to ~1.000, with complete coverage across 99.8% of training rows.** This verification confirms protocol resolution and establishes the automated check missing from earlier iterations.\n\n> **Methodological Takeaway.** Automated pipeline tests verify *internal consistency*, not *physical plausibility*. Cross-validation executed without error, models produced standard outputs, and all values were valid floating-point numbers. Catching 15 µL of water in a 15 mL reaction required domain expertise.\n',
    41: '### 6.2 Log of Dataset Corrections Across Iterations\n\n| # | Defect | Detection Mechanism | Iteration |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Pure liquid modulators in **µmol labeled as mmol** (×1000 error) | Formic acid median yielded 54 g in a 2.5 mL reactor | 2nd |\n| 2 | Adding mmol to µmol **erased HCl signal** | Consequence of (1) | 2nd |\n| 3 | **Synthesis type omitted** as feature, acting as dominant confounder | MW 95.7% crystalline vs. HTS 11.0% | 2nd |\n| 4 | **1-propanol and DEF missing** from feature table | Solvent fractions summed to 0.789 | 2nd |\n| 5 | **Liquid modulator acting as bulk solvent** | SMP-0013: 1.35 mL propionic acid in 1.5 mL total | 2nd |\n| 6 | **HF grouped with HCl** despite distinct mineralizing behavior | Chemical rationale | 2nd |\n| 7 | 22 rows missing ionic radius due to **non-breaking space (U+00A0)** | `MgCl2 · 6H2O` failed string match | 2nd |\n| **8** | **Dilute aqueous solutions recorded in mL, parsed as µL** | 15 µL water in 15 mL synthesis | **3rd** |\n| **9** | **Glacial AcOH, H₂PO₄, and NH₄OH** dropped during ingestion | Volumetric balance audit | **3rd** |\n| **10** | Second column for **HF and TFA** unmapped | Duplicate column naming in Excel | **3rd** |\n',
    42: '### 6.3 Sample Uniqueness, Deduplication, and Oxide Artifacts\n\nInitial profiling revealed that total *row count* exceeded unique `sample_code` instances: **43 physical samples had multiple independently labeled PXRD scans** (as-synthesized vs. washed, re-measurements). Retaining duplicates would introduce data leakage across splits (§5.8).\n\nAll 43 cases were manually audited via `app/pxrd_explorer.py`. In addition, **70 AMORFO scans showing sharp metal oxide Bragg peaks were excluded**: expert review confirmed that labeling them "amorphous" introduces target noise, as diffraction demonstrates crystallinity — albeit of an unwanted oxide phase.\n\n**Excluded records were flagged rather than deleted.** Deselected rows represent genuine analytical measurements, so they were marked via `ml_include = \'NO\'`. This preserves **auditability and reversibility**: in scientific data management, deletion destroys experimental provenance, whereas semantic flagging retains complete evidence.\n',
    43: '### 6.4 Tannic Acid: Chemical Rationale Confirmed by Empirical Evaluation\n\n24 samples utilized **tannic acid**, a polyphenol. It represents the **only non-phosphonate chemistry** in the corpus, with undefined coordination denticity (~25 phenolic hydroxyls preclude a single discrete denticity descriptor).\n\n**The exclusion rationale is chemical first:** 24 observations are insufficient for an estimator to learn a distinct chemical regime, while forcing linker descriptors to accommodate incompatible molecular topologies. Empirical evaluation confirmed the hypothesis: removing tannic acid increased macro F1 from 0.495 to 0.527.\n\n> ⚠️ **Methodological Caution.** Excluding data solely *because it improves performance metrics* constitutes overfitting. Here, the order of operations was preserved: **chemical justification first, empirical confirmation second**, ensuring sound methodological validity.\n',
    44: '### 6.5 Dropped Features and Rationale\n\n| Feature | Rationale |\n|---|---|\n| `electronegativity_allen` | **Undefined** for lanthanides; Pauling scale covers all 20 target metals |\n| `reactor_volume_ml` | **Constant** across HTS (2.5 mL): zero variance, zero predictive value |\n| `fill_fraction` | With constant reactor volume, identically equals `total_volume_ml / 2.5`: perfect collinearity |\n\nThe latter two were eliminated in this revision. In prior iterations, they persisted because zero-variance features do not raise execution exceptions. While tree estimators ignore uninformative constants, dropping them declutters feature importance rankings.\n',
    46: '### 6.6 Imputation Strategy: Within-Pipeline Validation\n\nWe impute numerical features using the **median** and categorical features using the **mode**, strictly **encapsulated within cross-validation folds**.\n\n> Encapsulation prevents subtle data leakage. Computing global medians prior to partitioning leaks test-fold distribution statistics into training folds. Within a `scikit-learn` pipeline, each fold computes medians strictly from its training split.\n\n**Evaluating explicit missingness indicators (`add_indicator`):**\n\n| Strategy | Macro F1 | MCC |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Random Forest, simple median imputation | 0.496 | 0.274 |\n| Random Forest, **with missingness indicator** | 0.495 | 0.266 |\n\nAdding indicators provides no performance gain; hence, standard imputation is retained. Documenting this negative result confirms an empirical design choice rather than an unverified assumption.\n',
    47: '---\n\n# §7. Modeling\n\n> *Stage 7 — Modeling. With a rigorous problem formulation and clean data pipelines, model training is concise and standardized.*\n\n### 7.1 Experimental Benchmark Design\n\n**We evaluate 10 model families** across diverse inductive biases, comparing linear, instance-based, kernel, and tree ensemble architectures.\n\n| Model | Inductive Bias / Decision Mechanism | Benchmark Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Majority Baseline** | Predicts most frequent class unconditionally | **Performance Floor**: minimum bar to beat |\n| **Logistic Regression** | Linear hyperplane separation | Interpretable linear baseline |\n| **KNN** | Local neighborhood voting | Non-parametric local estimator |\n| **Naive Bayes** | Feature independence given class | Performance drop measures collinearity impact (§5.6) |\n| **SVM (RBF)** | Non-linear maximum-margin hyperplane | Robust in moderate dimensions with limited samples |\n| **Decision Tree** | Orthogonal recursive binary partitioning | Interpretable non-linear tree base |\n| **Random Forest** | Bagging of decorrelated decision trees | Robust ensemble, low hyperparameter sensitivity |\n| **HistGradientBoosting** | Sequential gradient boosting | **Native missing value support** |\n| **LightGBM** | Leaf-wise gradient boosting | High-efficiency gradient boosted benchmark |\n| **XGBoost** | Regularized gradient boosted decision trees | Standard industry ensemble benchmark |\n\nAll supporting estimators incorporate `class_weight=\'balanced\'` to offset the 5.8:1 class imbalance by penalizing minority-class errors.\n\n### 7.2 Group-Aware Cross-Validation\n\n5-fold `StratifiedGroupKFold` enforces two simultaneous constraints:\n\n- **Grouped by plate**: no synthesis plate spans both train and test splits, preventing batch-signature memorization.\n- **Stratified**: preserves global class proportions across all five folds.\n\n### 7.3 Complete vs. Actionable Feature Sets\n\nWe address a fundamental domain consideration regarding **synthetic actionability**:\n\n> *The linker does not simply modify crystallization probability; the linker defines **the target material identity**. Altering the linker changes the chemical system rather than optimizing a reaction condition.*\n\n| Feature Category | Features | Experimental Actionability |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Identity / Design** | Metal and linker intrinsic properties | Defines target material; changing it **shifts research scope** |\n| **Process / Medium** | [M], L/M, temperature, time, ramps, modulators, solvents | **Directly tunable** within a fixed chemical system |\n\nRecommending "switch to LIG-D" is not actionable reaction optimization; it recommends exploring a different compound. Consequently, we train **two separate pipelines**, where **the performance divergence between them provides domain insight**.\n',
    50: '---\n\n# §8. Evaluation\n\n> *Stage 8 — Evaluation. Translating statistical performance metrics into laboratory utility and physical interpretation.*\n\n### 8.1 Metric Selection Under Class Imbalance\n\n| Metric | Formulation | Selection Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Macro F1** | Unweighted arithmetic mean of per-class F1 | Primary metric. Heavily penalizes poor minority-class recall |\n| **Balanced Accuracy** | Mean recall across all classes | Domain-interpretable: "identifies X% of crystalline samples" |\n| **MCC** | Matthews Correlation Coefficient (−1 to +1) | Highly stringent: evaluates to 0 under chance agreement |\n| **Standard Accuracy** | Total correct predictions / total samples | **Diagnostic baseline only**: majority-class baseline yields ~64% |\n\n> Note: **MRE, RMSE, and R² do not apply** to multi-class classification problems.\n',
    53: '### 8.2 Performance Analysis\n\n**1. Predictable signal is present but modest.** The top-performing complete model achieves **macro F1 ≈ 0.52** vs. **0.26** for the majority baseline (a 2x improvement), with MCC ≈ 0.31 indicating moderate statistical association.\n\n**2. Correcting volumetric units did NOT increase predictive accuracy.** The previous iteration (18% missing solvent data) achieved 0.522 macro F1, whereas the corrected dataset (99.8% complete) achieves 0.517. **The 0.005 difference is seven times smaller than cross-fold standard deviation (±0.036)**, representing statistically indistinguishable performance.\n\n> This provides an honest domain finding: completing solvent volume features **did not improve predictive power**. Two hypotheses exist: either bulk solvent composition exerts secondary influence relative to primary coordination kinetics, or its effect is governed by unrecorded variables (such as pH). The primary gain remains data integrity: **features now accurately reflect physical quantities**, enabling valid chemical interpretation.\n\n**3. The actionable model drops significantly.** Excluding metal and linker identity features reduces top macro F1 to **0.393** (MCC ≈ 0.10) — exceeding random baseline, but limited.\n\n> **Chemical Interpretation:** in this historical dataset, **the model primarily discriminates between metal-linker chemical systems rather than tuning conditions.** This does not imply process conditions are negligible; rather, process parameters were not varied across sufficient ranges within individual systems to establish strong learning signals (§5.5: ~13 temperatures, ~9 durations).\n\n**4. Naive Bayes performs below baseline.** This is expected: it assumes feature independence, whereas §5.6 demonstrated strong collinear feature blocks. **Its failure quantifies the impact of feature interdependencies.**\n',
    56: '### 8.3 Error Distribution Analysis: Ordinal Confusion Dynamics\n\nEvaluating error directionality reveals that **ordinal classification behavior holds asymmetrically**:\n\n**For amorphous samples, errors follow expected ordinal patterns:** misclassifications map predominantly to incipient crystallization (a matter of degree on raw diffractograms subject to expert boundary variance). Severe misclassifications (amorphous predicted as crystalline) remain rare.\n\n**For crystalline samples, error dispersion is wider.** While adjacent incipient predictions dominate, misclassifications predicting **amorphous** occur at **twice the rate** of the reverse error (15.6% vs. 7.9%). The model exhibits **lower sensitivity for the minority crystalline class**, occasionally failing to identify clear crystalline signatures. With 167 crystalline cases vs. 967 incipient, and crystalline outcomes representing a diverse structural landscape, this constitutes **the primary performance bottleneck**.\n\n**Implications for laboratory deployment (§9):** for screening prioritization (ranking candidate reactions on a plate), rank ordering matters more than exact discrete boundary assignment. However, this asymmetry refutes claims that severe classification errors are negligible.\n',
    58: '### 8.4 Chemical Interpretation: Functional Feature Blocks\n\n**Block-level aggregation rationale.** With collinear features, permutation importance distributes importance weights across correlated attributes. Evaluating `electronegativity_pauling` separately from `absolute_hardness_ev` would yield misleading attributions.\n\n**Reading the summary table.** The `sum` column indicates aggregate block importance; `mean` reflects **per-feature importance**. Both metrics are necessary because blocks vary in cardinality (solvent medium contains 22 features, whereas process conditions contain 4).\n\n- **Metal chemistry dominates overall and per feature** (sum 0.071 across 7 features). Primary drivers are ionic radius and charge/radius ratio — i.e., **cation charge density**, which controls metal-phosphonate coordination kinetics and determines whether precipitation yields ordered frameworks or amorphous solids.\n- **Process conditions rank second, nearly matching metal importance per feature** (0.039 sum across 4 features: 0.0099/feature vs. 0.0101/feature for metal). Temperature and duration carry weight, but narrow experimental variance (§5.5) constrains their aggregate contribution.\n- **Linker descriptors rank lower** (0.028 sum, 0.0046/feature): with only 4 distinct linkers in the dataset, available learnable variance is limited.\n- **Solvent medium ranks lowest per feature** (0.0011/feature, ~1/9th of metal chemistry), despite containing 22 features and achieving **complete data integrity** via §6.1. **Correcting data quality did not manufacture predictive signal.** Furthermore, 12 of 39 features show slightly negative permutation scores, indicating operational noise.\n\n> **Balanced Interpretation.** Low feature importance does not prove chemical irrelevance; it indicates that *these 22 composition features, given this sample size and model architecture*, provide minimal class separation. Unrecorded parameters (specifically pH) likely govern solution equilibrium.\n\n> ⚠️ **Permutation Importance Caution.** Under collinearity, permutation methods tend to **underestimate redundant features**, as remaining correlated features preserve signal. Absolute importance values should not be interpreted as absolute percentages of explained variance.\n',
    60: '**Interpreting the SHAP Summary Plot:** each point represents an individual synthesis. Horizontal position indicates SHAP value (impact on model output pushing toward CRYSTALLINE to the right, or away to the left). Color represents feature value (red = high, blue = low). Thus, *"red points to the right"* indicates that **high feature values favor crystallization**.\n\nSHAP values complement permutation importance: permutation quantifies *magnitude of importance*, while SHAP details **directionality and sample-level distributions**.\n',
    61: "---\n\n# §9. Model Deployment & Registry\n\n> *Stage 9 — Deployment. A predictive model confined to a notebook delivers no operational value.*\n\n### 9.1 Model Persistence Standards\n\n| Format | Extension | Stored Content | Appropriate Use Case |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **joblib / pickle** | `.joblib`, `.pkl` | **Full end-to-end pipeline** | Standard scikit-learn format. **Used in this project** |\n| **ONNX** | `.onnx` | Language-agnostic computation graph | Cross-platform production serving |\n| **Native** | `.txt` (LightGBM), `.json` (XGBoost) | Estimator weights only | Framework-specific version stability |\n| **Neural Weights** | `.pt`, `.safetensors`, `.keras` | Network weights and architecture | Deep learning workflows |\n\n`joblib` serializes standard Python objects but carries version coupling: loading across differing scikit-learn versions can alter inference behavior. Serializing the **complete pipeline** (including transformers and imputers) ensures raw feature ingestion without external preprocessing dependencies.\n\n#### Reproducibility Beyond Serialization\n\nInitial iterations achieved F1 0.62 but **cannot be reproduced today**: underlying training data definitions were updated. Loading legacy binary artifacts would execute without runtime exceptions while evaluating against obsolete feature schemas.\n\n> **A fully reproducible model requires six elements beyond the binary artifact:**\n>\n> | Component | Failure Mode Without Component |\n> |---|---|\n> | **1. Training Dataset** | Unknown training distribution (historical failure mode) |\n> | **2. Code Lineage** | Requires exact git commit hash |\n> | **3. Hyperparameters** | Cannot re-instantiate identical estimators |\n> | **4. Random Seed** | Yields non-deterministic variations |\n> | **5. Environment Versions** | Library defaults change across releases |\n> | **6. Serialized Binary** | Operational convenience; easily retrained if 1–5 exist |\n\n### 9.2 Database Model Registry Architecture\n\nImplemented via five tables in `scripts/sql/migrations/2026-08-29_ml_model_registry.sql`:\n\n| Table | Tracked Metadata | Separation Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ml_datasets` | SQL query, hash, row count, **group cardinality**, class distribution | Detects underlying data shifts under identical SQL |\n| `ml_models` | Algorithm, hyperparameters, pipeline stages, CV strategy, seed, commit, environment | Complete recipe specification |\n| `ml_model_metrics` | **Long-format metrics**: (model, metric_name, split_type, score) | Allows adding metrics without schema migrations |\n| `ml_model_features` | Included columns, feature roles, **excluded features and reasons** | Audit trail for feature provenance |\n| `ml_model_artifacts` | Serialized BLOB, byte size, SHA-256 hash | Isolates large binary payloads |\n\nBinaries are persisted in PostgreSQL to integrate with automated backup routines, and `is_reproducible = FALSE` requires mandatory rationale enforced via SQL `CHECK` constraints.\n\n```python\nfrom src.ml_model_registry import cargar_modelo\nmodelo = cargar_modelo(conn, 'crystallinity_v4_hts_full')\nmodelo.predict_proba(X_nuevas)     # warns if sklearn runtime version mismatches\n```\n\n> **Registry Metrics vs. Deployment Artifacts.** Registry metrics reflect **cross-validation estimates** (unbiased performance estimates), while serialized binaries represent estimators **retrained on the full dataset**.\n\n### 9.3 Deployment Readiness\n\n**Deliberately not deployed to autonomous production.** With macro F1 ~0.52 and MCC ~0.31, the model is suited for *experimental candidate ranking*, not autonomous decision-making.\n\n1. **Domain Interpretability (Ready)**: identifies key governing feature blocks and highlights missing experimental parameters.\n2. **Experimental Candidate Prioritization (Next Stage)**: ranking candidates for well-plate dispensing benefits from even moderate ranking signal over random selection.\n3. **Application Integration (Planned)**: exposing predictions with explicit uncertainty estimates.\n\n**Prerequisites prior to operational deployment:** nested cross-validation for hyperparameter tuning, probability calibration, and **temporal validation** (training on pre-2024 records to predict 2024–2025 outcomes).\n",
    63: '---\n\n# §10. Feedback Loop and Project Retrospective\n\n> *Stage 10 — Feedback. The feedback loop completing the data science cycle, operationalized through multiple iterations.*\n\n### Project Progression Summary\n\n| Dimension | Initial Baseline | Current State |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chemical Features | 20 metals and 6 linkers as **raw text** | 5 relational reference tables + `v_ml_features` view |\n| Data Quality | 6 undetected metal/precursor discrepancies | 0 discrepancies |\n| Record Uniqueness | 43 duplicate sample measurements | 1 row = 1 physical sample |\n| Modulator Units | **Factor of 1,000 error** | Corrected and verified via stoichiometry |\n| **Volumetric Units** | **Dilute solutions parsed as µL** | **Corrected; solvent balance closed at 99.8%** |\n| Method Confounders | Blended microwave, standard, and HTS | Filtered to HTS with documented rationale |\n| Missing Values | Zero-imputation masking missingness | Documented NULL semantics |\n| Uninformative Features | Constant and collinear columns retained | Dropped and documented |\n| Problem Feasibility | Unknown baseline | **Genuine predictive signal identified (macro F1 ~0.52)** |\n\n### Actionable Feedback for Laboratory Operations\n\n1. **Systematically vary process conditions.** Expanding thermal ramps and reaction durations **within fixed metal-linker systems** will provide variance required to learn kinetic parameters.\n2. **Systematically log reaction pH.** The primary unrecorded variable governing phosphonate deprotonation equilibrium.\n3. **Expand crystalline class labels.** The minority class (167 instances) represents the key target of interest.\n4. **Standardize volumetric units in spreadsheet templates.** Explicit header units prevent multi-unit ambiguity across columns.\n\n### Open Technical Limitations\n\n- Hyperparameter optimization, probability calibration, and temporal validation remain pending.\n- **Actionable model performance remains modest** (MCC ~0.10). Predicting outcomes within a fixed chemical system requires broader process parameter variance.\n- Modulator pKa and logP descriptors require literature citation tracking in `property_sources`.\n- Gallium oxophilicity remains permanently null (unrecorded in primary literature sources).\n\n### Core Conclusion\n\nThe limiting factor in this domain **is data variance, not algorithmic complexity**. Evaluating ten model families yielded a ceiling of macro F1 ~0.52. Model performance differences are small compared to the performance gains achievable by systematically exploring reaction parameter space.\n\n**Key Methodological Insight:** all ten major dataset issues were identified through **chemical domain inspection rather than statistical metrics**. Automated tests passed despite unit errors, missing solvents, and selection bias.\n\n> A machine learning model can achieve solid cross-validation scores while learning spurious artifacts. **The primary safeguard is expert domain validation of underlying data distributions**, supported by an iterative workflow that accommodates returns to data preparation stages.\n>\n> Correcting data quality does not guarantee higher predictive metrics, but ensures that models and interpretations reflect valid physical reality.\n',
}
